          <h4 class="example-heading">Report draft (first pass)</h4>
          <article class="prose-card">
            <p><strong>Title</strong>: The opportunities and challenges of social media and public discussion</p>

            <p>In recent years, social media has become an important space for many people to obtain information, express opinions, and take part in public discussion. Compared with traditional media, social media has a lower threshold; users can publish messages quickly and respond to others' views in real time, which makes public discussion faster and gives more people the chance to engage with issues that were otherwise hard to approach.</p>

            <p>However, while social media brings convenience, it also comes with some new problems. First, information spreads fast, but verification doesn't necessarily keep up; if unconfirmed messages are widely forwarded, misinformation may spread within a short time. Second, platform algorithms recommend similar content based on users' clicks, dwell time, and interaction records, which may mean users often see information close to their own position and encounter different viewpoints less often. In addition, discussion on social media is easily affected by emotion; short, strong, reaction-provoking content tends to be seen more easily than nuanced, complex content that takes time to understand.</p>

            <p>Even so, this does not mean that discussion on social media is all bad. Used well, social media can still become an important tool for public participation. For example, social issues can be seen by more people through social media, and marginalized groups may be able to speak up through online platforms. The problem does not lie in whether social media itself is absolutely good or bad, but in how users, platforms, and society jointly build better ways of judging information and better rules for discussion.</p>

            <p>From a personal viewpoint, social media is both a force that promotes public participation and a challenge that affects the quality of discussion. We might consider how, while enjoying the convenience and fast participation social media brings, we can stay alert in everyday use and try to understand different voices.</p>

            <p>In conclusion, facing public discussion on social media, we should not only ask "should we use social media", but should think further about how to judge whether information is reliable, how to avoid only ever encountering one perspective, and how to preserve room to think in a fast-response environment. These questions will affect whether we can turn social media into a space for higher-quality public discussion.</p>
          </article>

