          <h4 class="example-heading">Revised draft</h4>
          <article class="prose-card">
            <p><strong>Title</strong>: The opportunities and challenges of social media and public discussion</p>

            <p>In recent years, social media has gradually become an important core space for the public to obtain information, express opinions, and take part in public discussion. Compared with traditional media, social media has a lower participation threshold; users can not only publish messages quickly but also respond to others' views in real time. This characteristic makes the rhythm of public discussion considerably faster, and at the same time gives more people the chance to engage with social issues they would not easily have encountered.</p>

            <p>However, alongside the convenience and ubiquity social media brings, many new problems follow. First, although information spreads rapidly, verification often cannot keep pace, so unconfirmed messages, once widely forwarded, may cause misinformation to spread within a short time. Second, platform algorithms recommend similar content based on users' clicks, dwell time, and interaction records, which easily leaves users in a long-term information environment close to their own position, reducing the chance of encountering different viewpoints. In addition, discussion on social media is easily driven by emotion: compared with nuanced, complex content that takes time to understand, short, strong, reaction-provoking information tends to attract more attention.</p>

            <p>Even so, this does not mean all discussion on social media is negative. Used appropriately, social media can still be a powerful tool for advancing public participation. For example, many social issues have become visible to the public through online platforms, and marginalized groups have thereby gained a channel to be heard. The article points out that the key to the problem lies not in whether social media itself is absolutely good or bad, but in how users, platforms, and society jointly build sounder ways of judging information and rules for discussion.</p>

            <p>From a personal viewpoint, social media plays a double-edged role in public discussion. On one hand it opens up channels for diverse participation; on the other it brings tests such as information authenticity and echo chambers. While enjoying the convenience, we should perhaps also reflect on how to raise our information literacy in everyday use and avoid over-relying on information from a single position.</p>

            <p>In conclusion, facing the public-discussion environment social media has shaped, we should not remain at the binary question of "should we use social media", but should explore further: first, how can we effectively judge the reliability of information? Second, how do we avoid falling into the confines of a single perspective in everyday information intake? These directions of thought will be the key to whether social media can be turned into a space for higher-quality public discussion.</p>
          </article>

          <h4 class="example-heading">Summary of changes</h4>
          <ol class="numbered-list">
            <li><span class="numbered-list__num">1</span><span class="numbered-list__text">Restructured: expanded the draft and divided it into clear paragraphs, covering in order the topic introduction, the key-point summary (challenges and opportunities), the personal viewpoint, and follow-up questions tied to the article's ending.</span></li>
            <li><span class="numbered-list__num">2</span><span class="numbered-list__text">Elevated tone: adjusted the original draft's more colloquial expressions (such as "many people use it" and "I think") into objective narration better suited to a formal report.</span></li>
            <li><span class="numbered-list__num">3</span><span class="numbered-list__text">Added content and follow-up questions: based on the article, filled in key points such as algorithmic echo chambers, emotional content, and the shared responsibility of users, platforms, and society, and included 2 concrete follow-up questions at the end.</span></li>
            <li><span class="numbered-list__num">4</span><span class="numbered-list__text">Kept within limits: used only viewpoints and illustrations mentioned in the article throughout, with no outside material added.</span></li>
          </ol>

          <h4 class="example-heading">Items still needing human verification</h4>
          <ol class="numbered-list">
            <li><span class="numbered-list__num">1</span><span class="numbered-list__text">Please confirm whether the length and report requirements are appropriate (if there is an upper or lower limit, adjust the length as needed).</span></li>
            <li><span class="numbered-list__num">2</span><span class="numbered-list__text">Whether the personal-viewpoint section matches your real stance; if adjustment is needed, you can fine-tune the tone or add your own views.</span></li>
          </ol>
        </div>
      </details>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        This draft_v2 <strong>only appears on screen — it isn't saved to a file automatically</strong>. The <code>@</code> syntax can only read files, never write them. To keep this version, copy it out and save it yourself — the <code>data</code> folder still contains only the original three files.
      </p>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Also worth noting: point 1 of the summary of changes says it "divided it into clear paragraphs", but the actual output never added any subheadings. <strong>What the AI says about its own work also needs checking by you.</strong>
      </p>

      <div class="highlight-card">
        <p class="highlight-text">
          This exercise lets the AI participate in revision,<br/>
          but <strong>it doesn't make every judgment for you</strong>.<br/>
          Its job: keep the original ideas, improve structure, flag gaps, produce a version you can revise further.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- ============ Section: why we can revise ============ -->
      <h2 class="detail-heading">Why is revision OK here?</h2>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        When we cleaned up CSV data earlier, we recommended <strong>not</strong> letting the AI modify the original file directly. Source files are often <strong>sign-up sheets, survey results, rosters, or other records</strong>, and a wrong AI edit can have real-world consequences. Revision is OK here because:
      </p>

      <ul class="check-list">
        <li>What's being revised is a <strong>draft</strong>, not the source article</li>
        <li>What's being revised is <strong>phrasing and structure</strong> — not facts being invented out of thin air</li>
        <li>The result still <strong>needs human verification</strong></li>
        <li>The original files remain in place, <strong>so we can compare back</strong></li>
      </ul>

      <!-- ============ Section: what NOT to let AI do ============ -->
      <h2 class="detail-heading">Things the AI shouldn't decide on its own</h2>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        In a reading-and-writing workflow, a few things shouldn't be handed to the AI:
      </p>

      <ul class="role-list">
        <li class="role-item">
          <span class="role-item__label role-item__label--ord">1.</span>
          <span class="role-item__desc"><strong>Don't let the AI pretend it's read material you didn't provide.</strong> If the file isn't there, don't let the AI fill it in.</span>
        </li>
        <li class="role-item">
          <span class="role-item__label role-item__label--ord">2.</span>
          <span class="role-item__desc"><strong>Don't let the AI fabricate citations.</strong> If you're citing a real article, book, or website, verify it yourself.</span>
        </li>
        <li class="role-item">
          <span class="role-item__label role-item__label--ord">3.</span>
          <span class="role-item__desc"><strong>Don't let the AI decide your stance.</strong> It can offer angles to consider, but the final view is yours.</span>
        </li>
        <li class="role-item">
          <span class="role-item__label role-item__label--ord">4.</span>
          <span class="role-item__desc"><strong>Don't let the AI rewrite the original into a different meaning.</strong> The source article is a reference, not a draft.</span>
        </li>
        <li class="role-item">
          <span class="role-item__label role-item__label--ord">5.</span>
          <span class="role-item__desc"><strong>Don't take the AI's output as final.</strong> Always do a check before any formal use.</span>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <!-- ============ Section: prompt checklist ============ -->
      <h2 class="detail-heading">A small checklist for designing prompts</h2>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Before writing reading-and-writing prompts, ask yourself a few questions:
      </p>

      <ol class="numbered-list">
        <li><span class="numbered-list__num">1</span><span class="numbered-list__text">Which <strong>article</strong> should the AI read?</span></li>
        <li><span class="numbered-list__num">2</span><span class="numbered-list__text">Are there any <strong>assignment requirements or writing rules</strong>?</span></li>
        <li><span class="numbered-list__num">3</span><span class="numbered-list__text">Is this step about <strong>organizing, planning, drafting, or checking</strong>?</span></li>
        <li><span class="numbered-list__num">4</span><span class="numbered-list__text">Can the AI <strong>add outside information</strong>?</span></li>
        <li><span class="numbered-list__num">5</span><span class="numbered-list__text">What content needs to <strong>preserve original meaning</strong>?</span></li>
        <li><span class="numbered-list__num">6</span><span class="numbered-list__text">Where can it only <strong>suggest, not decide</strong>?</span></li>
        <li><span class="numbered-list__num">7</span><span class="numbered-list__text">Should the output be <strong>notes, an outline, a draft, or a list of revisions</strong>?</span></li>
      </ol>

      <div class="callout-card">
        <p class="callout-text">
          When these are answered ahead of time, <strong>the AI's responses tend to be much steadier</strong>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- ============ Section: takeaway ============ -->
      <h2 class="detail-heading">The most important idea on this page</h2>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        A reading-and-writing flow is <strong>not "hand the article to the AI and ask for the whole report."</strong> A more reliable approach is:
      </p>

      <div class="flow-card">
        <div class="flow-step">Read the article</div>
        <div class="flow-arrow">↓</div>
        <div class="flow-step">Take notes</div>
        <div class="flow-arrow">↓</div>
        <div class="flow-step">Match against the assignment</div>
        <div class="flow-arrow">↓</div>
        <div class="flow-step">Build the outline</div>
        <div class="flow-arrow">↓</div>
        <div class="flow-step">Produce a draft</div>
        <div class="flow-arrow">↓</div>
        <div class="flow-step flow-step--accent">Check and revise</div>
      </div>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        AI is good at organizing, reminding, checking, and rewriting drafts. But <strong>it shouldn't replace the reading itself, and it shouldn't decide your viewpoint</strong>.
      </p>

      <ul class="check-list">
        <li>The <strong>article</strong> is the source</li>
        <li>The <strong>outline</strong> is the route</li>
        <li>The <strong>draft</strong> can be revised</li>
        <li>The <strong>final version</strong> is yours to confirm</li>
        <li>The <strong>important calls</strong> shouldn't be left to the AI</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="highlight-card highlight-card--final">
        <p class="highlight-text">
          The AI is the assistant,<br/>
          not the writer.<br/>
          <strong>You still do the reading, the writing, and the judging.</strong>
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

